those  games this is a video about  zelda and just like mario nintendo's  second most popular franchise made a  perfect transition into the third  dimension  blowing the competition out of the water  with ocarina of time  but as is with all bold new changes not  everyone prefers the new direction  to this day a link to the past is still  considered by many  to be their favorite zelda game of all  time and although ocarina adapted a lot  of the themes story beats and concepts  from its predecessor  it's undeniable that the experience is  radically different  luckily nintendo never forgot about the  old school approach and continues to  release zelda games in the classic style  alongside the 3d titles  the two different styles have co-existed  ever since but i think it's safe to say  that in terms of popularity the 2d  top down games have kind of been living  in the shadow of their bigger brothers  with the choice between more than 20  games and with the exception of the  classic games which still hold a lot of  nostalgic value for people  the majority of zela fans seem to prefer  the 3d installments over its top-down  counterparts  if they had to make a hard choice we  just don't see the same level of hype as  with the announcement of a 2d game  now i do realize that polls like these  don't represent the most accurate result  but i didn't want to draw conclusions on  the popularity of the two based on sales  numbers  because sales numbers are heavily  influenced by a lot of different factors  such as the popularity of the system it  released on how many systems it released  on  the time of release and the pricing as  an example link's crossbow training  actually sold more copies than skyward  sword but  i don't think anyone would claim that  crossbow training is the better game  now who am i kidding of course it's far  superior to skyward sword  joking aside though this obviously had  to do with the fact that the wii  was brand new at the time and that the  game was bundled together with the wii  zapper for a cheaper price  so in this case i think asking the  general public for their opinion  presents a more honest answer  and the margin of the polls don't lie  the 3d games  are by far more popular this is in stark  contrast to some of nintendo's other  franchises  as there are plenty of people who prefer  2d metroid over the prime series  or choose the classic side scroller  style of mario games over the 3d  platformers  anyway with everyone now focused on  breath of the wild too and the soon to  release hyrule warriors age of calamity  how does he even how does eva stay up  and he  is he's way too heavy oh i can't  excuse me like i said with everyone now  focused on these upcoming titles  i want to give some love to the other  side of the zelda fandom and discuss the  beauty of these games its game design as  well as the advantages and disadvantages  one has over the other  so without further delay let's dive into  the beauty of top down  zelda games first off i want to clarify  that although games like phantom  hourglass spirit tracks and a link  between worlds also use different  vantage points and camera angles i still  categorize these under the same umbrella  as the other top-down installments  since the majority of its gameplay is  still experienced from a bird's-eye  perspective  in order to prevent myself from going  all over the place  i've divided the subject matter into 6  different categories  accessibility difficulty combat story  dungeons  and exploration so let's start from the  top and work our way down starting with  for the average veteran gamer out there  getting into a 3d game  is no biggie especially for those who  grew up after the fifth generation of  consoles  but for the majority of the people on  the planet this is a whole different  story  just boot up breath the world and hand  over the controller to someone who has  little to no experience in gaming and  you will see how hard it is for them to  get a grip on it  3d games demand a lot more spatial  awareness not to mention the control  scheme is often a lot more complicated  the 2d titles are much more  straightforward and a lot easier to  comprehend for your average player  mind you this doesn't mean that the game  is easier to complete  something we'll get into next but only  two axes and a more clear overview of  where you are in the space around you is  definitely a lot less overwhelming and  thus  more accessible the simplicity of top  down zelda  is something i've always admired about  it there are of course games like a link  between worlds which require a lot more  spatial awareness due to his wall  merging mechanic  and for that reason alone i would never  recommend it to an unexperienced player  as their  first top down zelda game but it's still  a lot easier to get into than say  twilight princess or skyward sword  again easier to control not to finish  which leads into  the flip side of this easy to grasp  gameplay concept is that despite the  fact that the games look  a lot easier any veteran zelda player  out there knows that the opposite is  true  in most cases in fact up until breath of  the wild the 3d titles had been plagued  by a rather low  difficulty a little too low for my taste  actually  and even breath of the wild stops being  hard after  say 10 hours of gameplay the 2d games  have never been afraid to get really  difficult  and this can be both a blessing and a  curse for some the higher difficulty  might be a big turn off no matter your  preference though  i think there's a certain beauty to the  fact that these games aren't afraid to  pose a good challenge to any player  regardless of age or experience  with the exception of maybe the first  zelda on nes  the games generally don't start off too  hard if we take a link to the past for  example it isn't until the turning point  of the game after getting transported to  the dark world that the difficulty  suddenly ramps up significantly  enemies are suddenly way stronger and  deal a lot more damage  the dark world truly hits hard during a  first playthrough  but by that point you've already had  plenty of time to get some good practice  and with some perseverance you will be  able to deal with the challenges ahead  the only 3d title which comes close to  this is ocarina of time  and it's blatantly obvious just how much  inspiration this title took from a link  to the past  the child section of the game is pretty  easy but as soon as you become an adult  the game makes a radical change  dungeons are suddenly a lot bigger and  more complex enemies more dangerous  and even the overall atmosphere  communicates a certain message of  you're an adult now playtime is over  it's by no means as hard as a link to  the past dark world but definitely came  the closest  unless you count majora's mask which was  weird confusing and hard to get into  from the moment you booted up the game  but  yeah that's majira's mask for you but  even then most of the difficulty from  the 3d games comes from that spatial  awareness i was talking about earlier  and some of the puzzle elements that  come with it  but in terms of actual survival the top  down games are definitely in a league of  their own  my very first zelda game was ocarina of  time and even on my very first  playthrough i must have died only around  5 or 6 times  i didn't play a link to the past until  after majora's mask in the gap between  majora and waiting for the wind waker to  come out and  oh boy safe to say i died a lot  but after finishing it i was hooked and  immediately picked up link's awakening  right after  and finished both the oracle games as  well so that's 4  classic top down zelda games i tackled  in less than two years time  you can imagine that when the wind waker  came out the challenge of that game was  kind of underwhelming to say the least  coming off of ocarina of time majora's  mask and then  four very challenging 2d titles which  brings us to the next segment  the main culprit for the higher  difficulty found in the top down games  is death a lot of deaths  these games can be absolutely  unforgivable they are by no means among  the hardest games out there like  castlevania or ninja gaiden with the  exception of zelda 2 which we don't talk  about here but they  are miles ahead of the 3d games i think  this has to do with the whole z target  lock-on system  the enemies in the 3d titles are  generally designed and programmed to be  tackled one by one  i mean look at this what's this other  guy doing why isn't he attacking me  in the top-down games enemies don't care  they will gang up on you  and eat your hylian ass for breakfast if  you're not careful  for me personally this is one of the  most fun aspects of these types of zelda  games  you enter a room full of enemies and you  gotta make quick decisions  how do i navigate the room safely what  are my escape options which enemies  should i attack first  do i get rid of the weaker enemies to  clear some room to move  or do i go straight for the stronger  ones or the ones who litter the room  with projectiles  funnily enough this type of combat  challenge has also been proven to work  in the 3d games as well with places like  the savage labyrinth the cave of shadows  and more recently the trial of the sword  in breath of the wild  where you actually have to study the  room and plan your tactics ahead of time  instead of just mindlessly picking off  enemies one by one like in regular  dungeons  but for some reason this is mostly saved  as an extra optional challenge in the 3d  installments  another thing that contributes to the  combat being harder in the top down  games is that your window of  invulnerability  is a lot less lenient as is the hit stun  on enemies  if you're not careful or impatient you  can get combo to death  real quickly and some enemies cannot  just mindlessly be whaled on as they  have little or  even no hits done at all compare a dark  nut from the first game to the ones from  twilight princess or the wind waker and  it becomes clear just how brutal they  used to be  it's always moving and predicting where  they will go next and what your optimal  approach is without the risk of getting  in its path  is hard in the 3d games it's just a  matter of blocking and parrying at the  right moments which  honestly is more about patience than it  is about skill  i think you get the point so let's go  into something completely different  zelda games have historically been more  about gameplay first story second  and the 2d games definitely live and  breathe that mentality  the story in these installments can be  very interesting but at the same time it  also keeps it very simple  and because of its art style and  text-based conversations they are  definitely not on par with the other  games in terms of cinematics  the 3d games simply feel bigger and more  epic even if the total amount of  gameplay hours of a top down game is  the same or even longer seeing a cute  little sprite perform all these funny  little movements while you read the  dialogue box underneath  definitely has its charm but i can  totally see why people aren't as  invested in the story of these games  than they are with the others  however if there's one thing the  top-down games have over the rest  is that they are much more experimental  and dare to take a lot more risks  think about it the only 3d title that  doesn't take place in hyrule  is majora's mask i mean okay in skyward  sword hyrule technically hasn't been  founded yet but it's still in the same  geographical location  compare that to the 2d games which have  holodrum labrena  coal and island new hyrule and yes i  will even count hightopia  all brand new kingdoms to explore the  same thing goes for villains  only two of the 3d titles don't feature  ganondorf as the main bad guy  sure we have supporting villains like  girohim and zen but compared to the 2d  titles  this is still child's play vati bellum  malidus  varan general onox the nightmare from  link's awakening  the lady and a fair share of new  supporting villains like yuga and  chancellor cole  it seems like in terms of variety and  story narrative and characters the 2d  games are the place to be  and i don't exactly know why perhaps  because nintendo views the 3d titles as  the more important  mainline games now they are more afraid  to step away from the norm and take  risks than they do with the other games  it's hard to say for sure this also goes  for certain gameplay elements  there's a lot of talk about breath of  the wild 2 possibly featuring co-op mode  between link and zelda which would be a  first for the 3d titles  meanwhile the 2d games have been  experimenting with multiplayer since the  first four swords and if breath the  while 2 doesn't feature co-op but rather  sees you switching between link and  zelda that too  has already been done to a certain  degree in spirit tracks  granted this last aspect doesn't really  have a whole lot to do with the story  specifically  but i didn't know where else to include  it so i thought i'd just mention it here  dungeons are a very subjective matter  when it comes to zelda  i am personally torn in terms of which  style i like the most  though i will say that the imposing size  and complexity of the 3d dungeons does  speak to me just a little bit more but  not by a large margin  there are plenty of 2d dungeons which i  vastly prefer  over some of the 3d ones however the  biggest strength of the 3d dungeons is  the variety in their aesthetics and  atmosphere  with the more recent top-down titles  this definitely has improved and  dungeons no longer feel like simple  palette swaps but i'd still give the  edge to the 3d  installments in that department there's  just something about experiencing a  dungeon from all angles that makes it  stand out more  but again like with the story the 2d  games have been a lot more experimental  in terms of actual dungeon mechanics  if i look back at just how crazy these  dungeons can get sometimes  i can't help but feel inspired as  someone who loves talking about game  design  so many different mechanics which  completely change the way you approach  these challenges  making an entire floor crash into the  floor below to access new areas  throwing ice down a shaft to freeze the  floor below using certain switches to  loop around the dungeon  having to travel between two different  dimensions to progress further  some dungeons are about finding loads  and loads of keys and  not all keys are required to beat the  dungeon whereas others barely feature  any keys at all i don't know why but it  just feels a lot easier to get lost than  having to think outside the box in these  dungeons than it is in the 3d  installments  in those games it can feel a lot more  formulaic at times  emphasis on most of the time there are  definitely exceptions  additionally sometimes dungeons in the  2d games also feature multiple exits and  entrances  which in the example of skull woods the  dungeon is entirely designed around  something that's a lot more scarce in  the other games and is never utilized to  this extent  in general there's also a lot more  optional rooms in these dungeons as well  and you know i get it designing an  entirely optional 3d space which the  player might never visit can feel like a  waste of precious development time  but that doesn't take away from the fact  that this does make the dungeons in the  top-down games feel a lot more of a  challenge  overall again i want to emphasize that  these are generalizations  there are very simple and  straightforward 2d dungeons and very  complex 3d ones  last but not least i want to touch upon  exploration  now i could never say with a straight  face that the exploration in any of the  2d games is better than the one  found in breath the wild at the same  time the reason why breath the wild's  exploration is so amazing is exactly  because it wanted to capture the feeling  of the first game on the nes  so there's that but aside from breath of  the wild a lot of the overworld  exploration in the 3d games before  was heavily toned down skyward sword  alright to be fair though this isn't  entirely the fault of the games  themselves  3d games demand a lot more power in  terms of hardware and up until breath of  the wild the technology just  wasn't there the worlds needed to be  bottlenecked and divided up into chunks  to make it work  but if there's anything the top-down  games have over its 3d brothers it's  that the secrets feel a lot  more secret like especially in the  earlier games because each screen was  more self-contained it was much easier  to hide stuff in very random spots  without making it impossible for the  player to find  caves chests and sometimes even a  dungeon could be hidden under a  seemingly random bush  rock or inside of a wall and because  you're viewing the world from a bird's  eye perspective  the game designers have an easier time  placing subtle hints in the terrain  assets  something that's a lot harder when  you're viewing the world from behind the  protagonist or from first person  try hiding a big secret in a random bush  or patch of grass in breath of the wild  yeah that'll go over well in general  it's a lot harder to miss stuff in the  top down games  since all the information is laid out in  front of you whereas in breath of the  wild you could walk by a hillside  not realizing there's something  significant right behind it  which is why in the case of breath of  the wild the zelda team expertly  implemented high vantage points and  paragliding to spot these secrets from  afar  in the 3d installments before breath of  the wild nintendo needed to be  a lot more obvious with their secrets  there are some very well hidden ones in  ocarina of time but because hyrule field  is so small and barren you're still a  lot less likely to miss them  additionally if you had a rumble pack  you had the option to use the stone of  agony to track these secrets down  and because some of these secrets would  have been close to impossible to find  without the rumble feature  none of the rewards are really all that  special and only of real interest to the  hardcore completionists out there  all in all i think it's a wonderful  thing that the 2d games still have their  place in the zelda franchise  they are not comparable to the mainline  3d titles  and that is exactly why these games  should always make their stay  sure they might not generate the same  kind of hype we find with the other  games  but whether you like it or not this is  how zelda started and how it pulled  millions of people into the fandom  as of late i've become a bit worried  about the 2d installments  even after the series made its  transition into 3d there was a time  especially in the 2000s that we got a  constant influx of amazing top-down  zelda games  we had oracle of ages and seasons in  2001  minish cap four swords and four swords  adventures in 2004  phantom hourglass in 2007 with spirit  tracks following less than two years  later in 2009  then things started slowing down a bit  more with a link between worlds in 2013  and the last brand new top down zelda  game we've gotten since then was  triforce heroes in 2015 and yeah we had  link's awakening in 2019 but that's not  a brand new zelda game but rather a  remake of a game from 1993  i'm not necessarily against remix but i  think i'm not alone in saying that i  would have vastly preferred a new zelda  game in this style than a remake of a  game i  already played decades ago not only that  but by remaking link's awakening  nintendo is setting themselves up for  having to remake the oracle games and  maybe even minish cap now too  development time which i would rather  see go towards a new experience  and yes i know these games are being  developed by a third party but that's  not really an excuse capcom which is  also a third party developed three  amazing top-down zelda games under the  direction of nintendo 4 if you also  count the first four swords game which  came bundled with diminish cap  now that the 3ds has been discontinued  and the switch basically merged  nintendo's handheld and console market  i sincerely hope that the classic zelda  style of games aren't thrown by the  wayside and demoted to mostly remakes  a good brand new top-down adventure is  always a great experience  and an excellent way to fill the void  between the 3d installments which  usually only release every four to five  years or so  and now for a channel update because man  my voice is really  starting to go as some of you with a  sharp eye may have noticed there's now  an additional button underneath my  videos  and on my channel page litwooden  oh wait uh join that's right the gods of  the google sphere have deemed me  worthy for channel memberships so if  patreon isn't really your bag but you  still want to support the channel  financially without having to go to a  third party website then this option is  now available  the memberships include similar perks  like getting your name into credits  alongside my patreons early access to  certain videos  if the deadline is lenient enough and in  the future i will do some behind the  scenes stuff as well like showing you  how i make some of my videos like the  hero's path for instance  and maybe even little tutorials since as  an example i get a lot of questions  about how the 3d map of wrath the wild  works in my animations  but of course what really matters is you  will gain access to 4 different emojis  to use in the comment section of my  videos and community posts  these include cat dog  bald mummy ganondorf and of course we  cannot go without the zone iced whirl  as of right now i'm restricted to only  four but these will be expanded later  down the line as i gain more members  again i want to make it perfectly clea